1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,921
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,921 --> 00:00:08,515
This is chapter four: builds, release flags and preview features.

3
00:00:08,965 --> 00:00:13,250
A surprising share of Java production failures aren't code at all.

4
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:20,847
They're build configuration: something left undeclared and inherited from whatever machine happened to run the build.

5
00:00:21,747 --> 00:00:22,996
Three failures.

6
00:00:22,996 --> 00:00:28,820
Works on my machine: a developer builds on Java 25 and CI fails on 21.

7
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:35,145
Or worse, both pass and production fails, because nothing declared a target.

8
00:00:35,595 --> 00:00:37,755
A NoSuchMethodError at runtime.

9
00:00:37,755 --> 00:00:42,564
Two libraries depend on the same vendor client at different versions.

10
00:00:42,564 --> 00:00:48,139
One of them won, silently, and it wasn't the one your code was compiled against.

11
00:00:48,589 --> 00:00:51,158
And a preview feature that escapes.

12
00:00:51,158 --> 00:01:00,407
Compiled with enable preview, then deployed onto a runtime without the flag, or onto a different JDK where it will never load.

13
00:01:01,307 --> 00:01:06,129
There are three separate version decisions, and they're often confused.

14
00:01:06,129 --> 00:01:08,302
First, which JDK runs the build.

15
00:01:08,302 --> 00:01:13,531
Declare it with a toolchain, rather than inheriting whatever the machine has.

16
00:01:13,981 --> 00:01:16,577
Second, which Java version you target.

17
00:01:16,577 --> 00:01:21,085
That's the release flag, covering language, bytecode, and the API.

18
00:01:21,535 --> 00:01:28,606
And third, which JDK runs in production: the container image, pinned to a patch version.

19
00:01:28,606 --> 00:01:32,704
You can build on 25, target 21, and deploy onto 21.

20
00:01:32,704 --> 00:01:37,365
What breaks things is leaving any of the three undeclared.

21
00:01:37,815 --> 00:01:47,673
Gradle's toolchain is the strongest form: it downloads the declared JDK if it's missing, so a fresh machine produces the same result.

22
00:01:47,673 --> 00:01:52,343
Maven toolchains need the JDK already installed and registered.

23
00:01:53,243 --> 00:01:55,015
Here's the conflict.

24
00:01:55,015 --> 00:02:02,459
The app depends on vendor client 1.0 directly, and on dispatch SDK, which needs 1.2.

25
00:02:02,909 --> 00:02:11,518
Maven picks the version nearest the root, so 1.0 wins, and ties at the same depth go to declaration order in the POM.

26
00:02:11,518 --> 00:02:16,153
Gradle picks the highest version anyone asked for, so 1.2 wins.

27
00:02:16,153 --> 00:02:18,508
Neither considers compatibility.

28
00:02:18,508 --> 00:02:27,485
The same graph can resolve differently in each tool, and the failure is a NoSuchMethodError at runtime, not a build error.

29
00:02:28,385 --> 00:02:30,474
To diagnose, ask the tool.

30
00:02:30,474 --> 00:02:38,268
Maven's dependency tree with an includes filter shows that 1.2 was omitted for conflict with 1.0.

31
00:02:38,268 --> 00:02:42,849
Gradle's dependency insight shows the same from its side.

32
00:02:43,299 --> 00:02:45,655
Then state the version explicitly.

33
00:02:45,655 --> 00:02:54,943
Gradle constraints, or Maven's dependency management, set the version without adding a dependency, and the because clause records why.

34
00:02:54,943 --> 00:03:00,835
Adding a direct dependency to fix a conflict works by accident, and hides the reason.

35
00:03:01,735 --> 00:03:05,527
A BOM declares a set of versions that work together.

36
00:03:05,527 --> 00:03:09,756
Import it, then omit the versions on individual artifacts.

37
00:03:09,756 --> 00:03:16,028
That's what prevents mixing JUnit's API from one release with its engine from another.

38
00:03:16,478 --> 00:03:19,539
And a reproducible build needs four things.

39
00:03:19,539 --> 00:03:21,177
Pinned plugin versions.

40
00:03:21,177 --> 00:03:27,371
No version ranges or snapshots, because then the artifact depends on when you built it.

41
00:03:27,371 --> 00:03:30,575
A fixed output timestamp for the JAR entries.

42
00:03:30,575 --> 00:03:36,057
And the wrapper, so everyone, including CI, uses the same build tool version.

43
00:03:36,057 --> 00:03:40,542
The Maven enforcer plugin can check the first two mechanically.

44
00:03:41,442 --> 00:03:45,300
Using a preview feature needs the flag in three places.

45
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:47,686
The compiler, and the test runner.

46
00:03:47,686 --> 00:03:56,525
Forgetting surefire is the usual mistake: compilation succeeds, and every single test fails with preview features not enabled.

47
00:03:56,975 --> 00:03:58,575
And third, at runtime.

48
00:03:59,025 --> 00:04:01,515
Put all of it behind a Maven profile.

49
00:04:01,515 --> 00:04:08,113
Then removing preview is one commit, and a normal build proves the code still compiles without it.

50
00:04:09,013 --> 00:04:14,016
Here's the constraint that decides whether you can use preview at all.

51
00:04:14,016 --> 00:04:24,593
A preview class file carries minor version sixty-five five three five, and refuses to load on any JDK other than the exact release that produced it.

52
00:04:24,593 --> 00:04:26,380
Not twenty-five or later.

53
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:27,237
Twenty-five.

54
00:04:27,237 --> 00:04:33,098
That's deliberate: it's what lets the JDK redesign a preview API between releases.

55
00:04:33,548 --> 00:04:39,340
So never publish a library with preview, and not an internal shared library either.

56
00:04:39,340 --> 00:04:44,225
A deployed service only with a pinned runtime and a plan to remove it.

57
00:04:44,225 --> 00:04:48,481
Scripts and spikes are fine, because rewriting them is cheap.

58
00:04:49,381 --> 00:04:57,211
Build in CI across a matrix of JDKs: the LTS you run in production, plus the latest release.

59
00:04:57,661 --> 00:05:06,948
The latest release surfaces deprecations, removals and behaviour changes months or years before an LTS upgrade forces them.

60
00:05:06,948 --> 00:05:11,101
Let that job fail without blocking merges, but read it.

61
00:05:12,001 --> 00:05:13,251
Production rules.

62
00:05:13,251 --> 00:05:18,618
Declare the build JDK with a toolchain, and target with the release flag.

63
00:05:18,618 --> 00:05:21,632
Commit the wrapper and use it everywhere.

64
00:05:21,632 --> 00:05:23,691
Pin plugins and import BOMs.

65
00:05:23,691 --> 00:05:26,852
Fix conflicts with constraints, saying why.

66
00:05:26,852 --> 00:05:30,088
Set parameters and UTF-8 in a shared parent.

67
00:05:30,088 --> 00:05:32,587
And keep preview behind a profile.

68
00:05:33,037 --> 00:05:37,263
Never use version ranges or snapshots in anything released.

69
00:05:37,263 --> 00:05:40,557
Never publish a library compiled with preview.

70
00:05:40,557 --> 00:05:43,135
Never forget the test configuration.

71
00:05:43,135 --> 00:05:46,787
Never fix a conflict by adding a direct dependency.

72
00:05:46,787 --> 00:05:51,299
And never let the IDE hold compiler settings the build doesn't.

73
00:05:52,199 --> 00:05:52,821
To recap.

74
00:05:52,821 --> 00:05:59,047
The build JDK, the target version and the production runtime are three separate decisions.

75
00:05:59,047 --> 00:06:00,292
Declare all three.

76
00:06:00,292 --> 00:06:07,970
Maven picks the nearest version and Gradle the highest, so state versions explicitly with BOMs and constraints.

77
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:12,743
And a preview class file only loads on the one release that built it.

78
00:06:13,193 --> 00:06:18,073
Next is chapter five: migration strategy, from Java 8 to 25.

79
00:06:18,073 --> 00:06:19,699
Thanks for watching.
